WEBVTT

00:00:00.603 --> 00:00:01.470
Hello everyone.

00:00:01.569 --> 00:00:03.390
Today we talk about nail shapes.

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:04.910
Shaping sounds simple;

00:00:05.019 --> 00:00:06.350
anyone can hold a file.

00:00:06.619 --> 00:00:08.912
But the shape decides whether the nails last,

00:00:08.862 --> 00:00:12.750
whether they suit the hand of the client, and whether she wants to come back.

00:00:13.088 --> 00:00:14.750
There are seven common shapes:

00:00:14.859 --> 00:00:20.990
square, squoval, round, oval, almond, coffin, and the pointed stiletto.

00:00:21.663 --> 00:00:23.331
Let us go through them one by one.

00:00:23.798 --> 00:00:24.531
Square:

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.918
the tip is filed straight across, the sides are straight,

00:00:27.868 --> 00:00:29.491
and the two corners are sharp.

00:00:29.835 --> 00:00:31.811
Squoval, as we call it in the trade:

00:00:32.153 --> 00:00:36.131
still straight across like the square, but the two corners are gently rounded.

00:00:36.621 --> 00:00:37.331
Round:

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.931
a short nail, with the tip following the curve of the fingertip.

00:00:41.359 --> 00:00:41.891
Oval:

00:00:42.068 --> 00:00:45.107
a little longer, with both sides curving evenly,

00:00:45.057 --> 00:00:47.411
meeting in a rounded tip like an egg.

00:00:48.088 --> 00:00:48.706
Almond:

00:00:49.027 --> 00:00:53.301
both sides taper towards the middle, into a point that is soft and rounded,

00:00:53.251 --> 00:00:54.146
like an almond.

00:00:54.695 --> 00:00:56.866
Coffin, also called ballerina:

00:00:57.192 --> 00:01:01.067
the sides taper like an almond, then the tip is filed straight across,

00:01:01.017 --> 00:01:02.706
like the toe of a ballet shoe.

00:01:03.133 --> 00:01:03.906
And stiletto:

00:01:04.172 --> 00:01:07.506
both sides taper steeply to a sharp point right in the middle.

00:01:08.046 --> 00:01:09.282
Now, strength.

00:01:09.679 --> 00:01:10.802
Think of it like this:

00:01:11.151 --> 00:01:13.602
everyday knocks always land on the tip.

00:01:13.923 --> 00:01:17.202
The more material there is at the tip, the better it takes the force.

00:01:17.628 --> 00:01:20.756
Shapes that taper to a point, like almond and stiletto,

00:01:20.706 --> 00:01:22.882
leave very little material at the tip.

00:01:23.181 --> 00:01:26.387
So these shapes usually suit strong natural nails,

00:01:26.337 --> 00:01:28.722
or nails with gel or acrylic on them.

00:01:29.363 --> 00:01:30.658
Square is the opposite:

00:01:30.813 --> 00:01:32.578
the tip is wide and strong.

00:01:32.925 --> 00:01:36.898
But the two sharp corners catch on sweaters and hair, and chip easily.

00:01:37.295 --> 00:01:38.578
Something that happens a lot:

00:01:38.847 --> 00:01:42.417
a client loves the square shape, then comes back a few days later,

00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:44.178
because a corner has chipped off.

00:01:44.525 --> 00:01:44.947
Round,

00:01:44.897 --> 00:01:50.098
oval and squoval are the most practical and the strongest shapes for natural nails.

00:01:50.516 --> 00:01:53.138
That is also why so many clients choose squoval.

00:01:53.318 --> 00:01:56.978
Coffin needs length, so it is usually done on enhanced nails.

00:01:57.525 --> 00:02:00.104
So how do you choose a shape that suits the client?

00:02:00.355 --> 00:02:02.024
First, look at the hand.

00:02:02.448 --> 00:02:07.064
Long, slim fingers look good with almost any shape, even square.

00:02:07.488 --> 00:02:12.504
Short fingers and wide nails usually look longer and slimmer with oval or almond.

00:02:12.853 --> 00:02:16.184
And square on wide nails tends to make the fingers look shorter.

00:02:16.839 --> 00:02:19.628
Second, look at the curve at the base of the nail.

00:02:19.963 --> 00:02:21.468
Many technicians have a trick:

00:02:21.897 --> 00:02:25.548
the tip shape looks most harmonious when it repeats the curve at the base.

00:02:26.044 --> 00:02:28.268
A round base suits round or oval.

00:02:28.617 --> 00:02:30.748
A more square base goes well with squoval.

00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:33.468
This is not a law, just a good starting point.

00:02:34.054 --> 00:02:37.088
Third, ask about the work and the life of the client.

00:02:37.496 --> 00:02:40.693
For people who type a lot, work with their hands, cook,

00:02:40.643 --> 00:02:42.352
or look after small children,

00:02:42.302 --> 00:02:46.528
short nails in a round or squoval shape are usually the most comfortable.

00:02:46.953 --> 00:02:51.808
Many workplaces, like hospitals or kitchens, also have their own rules about nails.

00:02:52.241 --> 00:02:55.248
When a client mentions her job, ask one more question.

00:02:55.804 --> 00:02:58.774
Fourth, look at how strong the natural nails are.

00:02:59.114 --> 00:03:02.729
If they are thin, soft, or split into layers at the tip,

00:03:02.679 --> 00:03:06.294
do not file them to a point, and do not leave them long.

00:03:06.723 --> 00:03:10.134
A round shape, or a short oval, will last longer.

00:03:10.737 --> 00:03:13.456
If the client wants long almonds, or stilettos,

00:03:13.406 --> 00:03:18.054
it is better to do them on enhancements, as in the lesson on extensions on forms.

00:03:18.596 --> 00:03:22.305
Now, what separates a great technician from an ordinary one:

00:03:22.559 --> 00:03:23.585
consistency.

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:27.665
All ten nails must be the same length, and the same shape.

00:03:28.093 --> 00:03:31.445
If a single nail is off, the client sees it straight away,

00:03:31.395 --> 00:03:33.665
because she looks at her hands all day.

00:03:34.117 --> 00:03:39.185
Seen from above, the tip must sit right in the middle, with both sides symmetrical.

00:03:39.454 --> 00:03:41.905
Then turn the finger, and look from the tip:

00:03:42.077 --> 00:03:44.865
both sides must be balanced, and the curve even.

00:03:45.493 --> 00:03:47.191
Next, the side walls.

00:03:47.703 --> 00:03:49.091
On both sides of the nail,

00:03:49.041 --> 00:03:52.231
there is a point where the nail starts to leave the skin bed.

00:03:52.566 --> 00:03:54.871
In the trade, we call it the stress point.

00:03:55.204 --> 00:03:59.991
From there, the sides must run straight forward, and only then do you shape the tip.

00:04:00.336 --> 00:04:01.831
And a very common mistake:

00:04:02.101 --> 00:04:05.191
filing deep into the side corners of the natural nail.

00:04:05.433 --> 00:04:10.080
It makes the nail look slimmer, but the nail loses its support on both sides,

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:11.991
so it is weak and breaks easily.

00:04:12.393 --> 00:04:16.635
As the nail grows out, its edge can also dig into the skin at the corner,

00:04:16.585 --> 00:04:18.071
causing painful swelling.

00:04:18.733 --> 00:04:19.678
About the file.

00:04:20.056 --> 00:04:22.798
The higher the number on the file, the finer it is.

00:04:22.965 --> 00:04:27.118
For natural nails, use a fine file, usually 180 or higher.

00:04:27.543 --> 00:04:31.678
For hard gel or acrylic, use a coarser file, depending on the job.

00:04:32.109 --> 00:04:36.638
On natural nails, file in a single direction, as in the gel polish lesson.

00:04:36.987 --> 00:04:41.358
Sawing back and forth heats the nail, and the tip splits into layers easily.

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:44.081
Finally, be honest with the client.

00:04:44.255 --> 00:04:45.601
Something that happens a lot:

00:04:45.869 --> 00:04:49.224
a client brings a picture of very long, pointed nails,

00:04:49.174 --> 00:04:51.121
but her natural nails are thin.

00:04:51.547 --> 00:04:53.281
Do not just nod to please her.

00:04:53.709 --> 00:04:54.801
Explain gently:

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:58.721
on your natural nails, this shape will break very easily.

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:00.241
Then offer choices:

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:04.801
a shorter almond on the natural nails, or enhancements to make the pointed shape.

00:05:05.112 --> 00:05:07.521
When clients understand, they trust you.

00:05:07.757 --> 00:05:10.895
And remember to note the shape and length the client chose,

00:05:10.845 --> 00:05:13.041
so next time you can do exactly the same.

00:05:13.693 --> 00:05:14.332
You see.

00:05:14.566 --> 00:05:16.412
Shaping is not just about looks.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:20.322
It is choosing a shape that suits the hand, the natural nails,

00:05:20.272 --> 00:05:21.852
and the life of the client,

00:05:22.117 --> 00:05:24.172
then making all ten fingers match.

00:05:24.348 --> 00:05:28.012
In the practice lesson, we will file each shape, one by one.

00:05:28.268 --> 00:05:30.892
And now, take the short quiz below the video.

00:05:30.983 --> 00:05:31.852
See you next time.
